<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/intermediate/your-first-1000-words">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Vocabulary is the rate-limiting resource of every early learner: grammar you can dodge,
    pronunciation you can approximate, but an unknown word is an unknown word. And the
    first 1,000 words are <strong>the highest-yield learning investment in the entire
    project</strong>, because languages distribute their work absurdly unevenly — a few
    hundred words do most of the talking. This lesson is the strategy for harvesting that
    distribution: what to learn first, how to make it stick, and how to know when you have
    enough to switch from studying to simply consuming the language.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also maps onto the A1 stage of the CEFR ladder: "understand and use familiar
    everyday expressions", "introduce yourself and others", "ask and answer questions
    about personal details", "interact in a simple way provided the other person talks
    slowly and clearly". Every one of those can-do statements is a vocabulary problem
    first — greetings, question words, numbers, family terms, a few dozen verbs — and the
    fastest A1s are built exactly the way this lesson describes: high-frequency words,
    spaced review, sentences from day one.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="frequency, spacing, collocations — the vocabulary engine" widget="equationStep" config={freqCfg} />

  <h2>The coverage curve, with numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Word frequency follows <strong>Zipf's law</strong>: a word's frequency is roughly
    inversely proportional to its rank. The top 100 words of English (the, be, to, of,
    and, a, in…) account for about half of running text; add ranks 101–1,000 and you are
    near 70–85% of ordinary speech (written text is a bit more demanding). The curve then
    flattens: 2,000 words buys roughly 85%, 5,000 about 92–95%, and comfortable unassisted
    reading of novels needs about <strong>98% coverage</strong> — in practice 8,000–9,000
    word families for English, which is what educated native speakers carry (15,000–20,000
    for the full range). The 98% figure is the practical threshold from vocabulary
    research: below it, unknown words come too densely to infer from context; above it,
    reading teaches itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    The curve explains the emotional shape of learning. The first months feel like
    magic — every session visibly unlocks conversations. The intermediate plateau is not
    failure but arithmetic: each doubling of vocabulary buys a smaller share, because the
    remaining words are rarer. Knowing the shape in advance is protective: you learn to
    measure progress in <em>coverage</em> (unknown words per hundred) rather than in
    feelings, and you expect the exchange rate to fall.
  </p>

  <h2>What the first 1,000 must contain</h2>
  <p>
    Frequency lists already encode the answer, but it is worth seeing the anatomy. The
    first thousand are dominated by <strong>function words</strong> (the, of, and, but,
    this, that), <strong>pronouns</strong>, <strong>high-frequency verbs</strong> (be,
    have, go, want, can, make, say), <strong>question words</strong>, <strong>numbers and
    time words</strong>, and a shell of concrete nouns and adjectives that cover daily
    life (water, house, mother, big, good). Together they carry the sentence skeleton —
    content words plug into them. A worked A1 objective: the self-introduction. With
    about twenty words (hello, name, I, am, from, city, live, work, family, have, two,
    brother, one, sister, like, music, and, but, very, thank-you) you can run the CEFR's
    entire "introduce yourself and ask and answer personal questions" descriptor —
    twenty words, one can-do.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two bundling rules multiply the yield. <strong>Learn grammatical baggage with the
    word</strong>: "la mesa", "el libro" — gender is free at first meeting and agonising
    to retrofit across 1,000 nouns; the same goes for irregular pasts ("went", not
    "go + ed" discovered later) and plural types. <strong>Learn the word's common
    company</strong>: frequency dictionaries and corpora publish collocations, and the
    difference between "make a decision" and the directly-translated *"do a decision" is
    the difference between native-seeming and translationese. Cards that carry a word plus
    its article plus one authentic sentence cost little extra and encode all three at
    once.
  </p>

  <h2>Spaced repetition: the retention engine</h2>
  <p>
    Memory research since Ebbinghaus (1885) gives two robust findings.
    <strong>Forgetting is exponential</strong> — retention falls steeply in the first days
    and then flattens — and <strong>each successful recall at the edge of forgetting
    flattens the next curve</strong>. Spaced repetition systems (Anki and relatives, built
    on Woźniak's SM-2 algorithm) exploit both: they schedule each card's next review just
    before you would likely forget it, and stretch the interval each time you succeed
    (one day, three days, a week, two weeks, a month…). A card you keep recalling after a
    year costs you seconds per year. The scheduling is individual — your failures reset
    <em>your</em> intervals — which is what makes the software better than any fixed
    timetable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The companion finding is the <strong>testing effect</strong>: attempting retrieval
    strengthens memory far more than re-reading. Flashcards are retrieval practice in a
    box, and the direction matters — target-language prompt, native answer, then flipped.
    Dosage: twenty minutes daily beats three hours weekly by an amount that surprises
    everyone who measures it (the spacing effect is one of psychology's most replicated
    results). And the limit is real: sustained vocabulary growth tops out somewhere
    around ten to twenty new words a day for a busy adult, of which some fraction fades
    anyway — which is precisely why spending that budget on high-frequency words is the
    only rational allocation.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring progress by coverage</h2>
  <p>
    The tactical metric: grab a native text or podcast transcript, sample a hundred words,
    count the unknowns. Under about ten unknowns per hundred, context starts doing real
    work — you can learn <em>from</em> the content instead of preparing for it — and that
    changes everything about your practice, because input becomes your main engine (the
    listening lesson takes over). Above that density, keep building vocabulary
    deliberately. Re-run the count monthly on fresh material. It converts "am I getting
    better?" into a number, and the number tells you which lesson of this course you
    should be living in this month.
  </p>

  <h2>Building the deck: card design that survives contact</h2>
  <p>
    The flashcard deck is infrastructure, and bad card design quietly kills more
    vocabulary projects than laziness does. The rules that survive practice:
    <strong>one fact per card</strong> (a card testing gender, meaning and spelling at
    once fails at its weakest member); <strong>target-language prompt by default</strong>
    (recognition is easier than production, and you need production); <strong>an example
    sentence on every content word</strong>, ideally harvested from your own input so the
    sentence is one you actually met; <strong>audio on everything</strong> (the
    pronunciation lesson's loop, pre-loaded); <strong>images for concrete nouns</strong>
    where cheap — a picture skips the translation middleman. Two maintenance disciplines:
    delete or rewrite cards that fail five times (leeches — the deck debugging itself),
    and retire cards of words you now meet constantly in input (real usage has replaced
    the drill — the goal all along). The deck is scaffolding, not the building; its job
    is to make itself unnecessary.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked build: the first 1,000 in ninety days</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the programme, with numbers. Days 1–30: pure frequency. New cards at 20 per
    day (reviews climb to ~100/day within two weeks; at 3–5 seconds each, reviews cost
    8–10 minutes). Days 31–60: keep 15–20 new frequency words per day, but harvest every
    tenth card from your own listening and reading — words you have already met in the
    wild stick at roughly twice the rate, because they carry context. Days 61–90: tilt
    to 50/50 frequency list versus mined words, and start testing coverage monthly. By
    day 90 you hold roughly 1,200–1,500 words (some fade — budget for it), your unknowns
    per hundred on learner podcasts have fallen from forty-odd to ten or fewer, and the
    listening lesson's band is now open to you. Total cost: twenty to thirty minutes a
    day. There is no trick that beats this budget; there is only a distribution that
    makes the budget count — which is the entire content of this lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>Function words: grammar in disguise</h2>
  <p>
    Look again at what the top of the frequency list contains: the, of, to, and, a, in,
    that, is, was, for, with. These are function words, and they are where vocabulary
    and grammar fuse — every one of them is a grammatical element wearing a word's
    clothes. Two consequences. First, front-loading them is also front-loading grammar:
    learning "is/are", "was/were", "of" and "to" early delivers the sentence skeleton
    before any conjugation table is opened (the grammar lesson's chunks ride on exactly
    these words). Second, they are the words flashcards handle worst — prepositions and
    articles resist translation because their meaning is relational. Learn them inside
    dozens of high-frequency phrases ("in the morning", "on Monday", "of course") rather
    than as isolated items, and let the input hours show you the system they encode.
  </p>

  <h2>The second thousand and the shape of the climb</h2>
  <p>
    The first 1,000 come from a list; the second 1,000 come from your own input, and the
    handover is the key transition in the whole project. Keep a capture file: every word
    you meet twice in real content goes in, and mined words (drawn from what you actually
    watch and read) replace list words at an accelerating rate — by word 2,000 the list
    is serving only as a quality check (is this word as common as my mined word?). The
    reason is efficiency: from here on, vocabulary stratifies by <em>your</em> genre —
    a podcast listener needs "episode", "subscriber" and "however"; a novel reader needs
    "glanced", "narrow" and "murmured" — and no generic list ranks those for your life.
    The climb continues to roughly 4,000–5,000 words before comfortable native media,
    each block bought slightly dearer than the last, with spaced repetition carrying the
    load and coverage counts confirming the slope. The first 1,000 started the engine;
    from here, the fuel is everything you understand — which was the point of buying
    those 1,000 first.
  </p>

  <h2>Words you already own: the Cognate and loanword dividend</h2>
  <p>
    Before the deck, collect the words you already have. Languages in contact share
    vocabulary — internationalisms (hotel, taxi, television, pizza in dozens of
    languages), loanwords flowing both ways (Japanese has thousands of English loans;
    English has thousands from French, Latin and every language it has touched), and
    for related languages whole families of cognates (Spanish-English: vista, padre,
    necesario — thousands of words related by sound correspondences you can learn as
    patterns rather than as items). For many language pairs this dividend is worth
    several hundred "free" words — real coverage that costs recognition practice only.
    Two cautions keep it honest: <strong>false friends</strong> are real (Spanish "embarazada"
    is not "embarrassed"; Japanese "handoru" is not "handle" in every sense — check the
    ones that matter), and pronunciation of loans follows the borrowing language's
    system, so hear them before you say them. Then start the frequency list from word
    one, knowing roughly where you actually started.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements for the strategy-minded. <strong>Word families versus
    lemmas</strong>: "run, runs, ran, running" is one word family but several lemmas, and
    frequency lists differ in which they count — a 1,000-word list of families is a
    different (larger) acquisition than 1,000 lemmas; know which list you are holding.
    <strong>Genre matters</strong>: the top 1,000 of conversation and the top 1,000 of
    news overlap by perhaps two-thirds; a learner aiming at podcasts should skim their
    frequency lists off spoken corpora, one aiming at novels off written ones.
    <strong>Core-vocabulary history</strong>: the idea of a minimal working vocabulary is
    old and battle-tested — Ogden's Basic English (1930) reduced English to 850 words and
    was taught to speakers of dozens of languages; modern controlled vocabularies (graded
    readers, Simplified English for aviation) descend from it. The consensus that emerged:
    a small core plus generous context can carry surprisingly much communication — but
    the core must be chosen by frequency, not by textbook chapter. That one decision,
    made early, compounds for years.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language (2001; 2nd ed. 2013); Hu &amp; Nation (2000) on the 98% threshold; Davies &amp; Gardner, A Frequency Dictionary of (many languages); Ebbinghaus (1885) and the spacing-effect literature.</p>
